nd hopefully adopt What's called the  global biodiversity framework as part of  the sister biodiversity cop and just  like the Paris agreement on climate we  now need an ambitious Global  biodiversity framework and the reason  that I'm saying that is because  businesses need the political certainty  to then scale and speed up action and if  we're really honest about it  unfortunately the voluntary actions are  just not enough to address the scale of  nature loss we're seeing and that has  impacts on people of course which should  be Central to these decisions  I should say then that because the  voluntary actions are not are not enough  what's happening now is that more than  330 companies and financial institutions  from 52 countries are calling for  mandatory requirements for all large  businesses and financial institutions to  both assess and disclose their impacts  and dependencies on nature so that's a  key element for us in that Global  biodiversity framework because without  this information yeah sorry I'm digging  right in there yeah they're really going  to get involved in this these businesses  then  they are and we've actually seen  tremendous momentum like never before  it's really unprecedented hundreds of  companies now finally seeing nature on  their business agenda alongside the  political one now these all of these  political decisions and Frameworks only  are impactful if they lead to action and  we can only take meaningful action if we  have the information so right now we  don't even have the information when we  say we it's even governments consumers  businesses investors don't have the  right information of the impacts and  dependencies that lead to risks and  opportunities for for all of us and so  that's why we're essentially Flying  Blind and it's completely normal to  require businesses to report on their  financial performance we now need there  be there to be mandatory requirements to  report on natural social and human  capital too  on a scale of one to ten how optimistic  are you that this Summit can achieve  anything previous commitments have  really not been met have they  that's and  if that point really tricky question  because a lot of the discussions are  actually quite up in the air right now  there are some positive signs of  discussions moving forward but again  um it's a bit too early to say I remain  optimistic that there will be  an agreement  um especially I think what's needed now  is that high level political leadership  coming in and so the there are over a  hundred ministers that are planning to  come to cop15 next week that needs to  unblock some of the negotiations  um on the ground  but it regardless of an agreement I  would just add I think that the momentum  that the cop 15 has provided as a hook  for realizing yes we need that high  policy ambition at a global level that  will then be implemented at a national  level and will set the bar that bar  needs to be set High to level the  playing field for all companies  um and essentially the message that  we're going in with hundreds of  companies that are ready for the change  is business as usual is no longer an  option and we need large-scale action  that can only happen if all elements of  the system's transformation are in place  so businesses don't operate in isolation  they operate within multiple systems  that we all as individuals are part of  with how we spend our time our money and  who we vote for for example all right  well we'll see what happens with me Eva  is Abby great to talk to you thanks for  your time  thank you  and that is it from us today just a  reminder though you can watch Tom heaps  climate show on Saturdays and Sundays at  3 30 in the afternoon and again it's 7  30 in the evening  and if you have questions about climate  change that you want answered then send  them all to us using this QR code and we  will try to answer as many as we can on  Tom's show but that's it for now and  from me thanks for wa